# SYNTAX TEST "text.html.tiddlywiki5" "An unparseable macro attribute value refuses the whole tag"

# parseutils.js ends parseAttribute with a deliberate refusal, and its comment names the intent:
# a value that starts with << and has a >> ahead but does not parse as a macro invocation returns
# null "so that the enclosing tag fails to parse rather than silently binding the attribute to
# true and treating the remainder as further attributes (restores v5.3.8 behaviour)".
#
# The macro name reads ([^\s>"'=]+), so << >> with nothing between them names nothing and the
# whole tag refuses. TiddlyWiki yields plain text for it; the grammar paints a widget.
#
# Closing this wants the widget's opening pattern to look past every attribute before it opens —
# a lookahead over an unbounded attribute list, where any one value may itself carry quotes,
# braces, backticks or nested angle brackets. The tag-name law next door narrows by a character
# class, which a lookahead can express; this one narrows by whether a later region parses, which
# it cannot.
#
# Measured cost of leaving it: an attribute value of << >> with no name, which a wiki reaches
# only mid-keystroke or by mistake.

<$link to=<<>>>go</$link>
# ^^^^ - entity.name.tag.html.tiddlywiki5
